container
└── /var/lib/mysql
    └── wordpress

mariadb_data          /var/lib/mysql
Docker volume    →    MariaDB data folder

So MariaDB writes its databases into persistent storage instead of the container's disposable filesystem. 💾


Without a volume:
    MariaDB container
    └── /var/lib/mysql
        └── wordpress database

    Delete container ❌
            ↓
    database can disappear 💀

With a volume:
    Docker Volume 💾
        ↕
    /var/lib/mysql
        ↕
    MariaDB container

---

Delete container ❌
      ↓
Volume still exists ✅
      ↓
Create new MariaDB container
      ↓
Attach same volume
      ↓
Database is still there 🎉

---

Container = disposable 🗑️, Volume = keeps important data 💾.

---

Inside mariadb service:
mariadb_data:/var/lib/mysql
       │
       └── "use this volume here"

Bottom of compose:
volumes:
  mariadb_data:
       │
       └── "define this volume"

----

volumes:
  mariadb_data:
    driver: local
    driver_opts:
      type: none
      o: bind
      device: /home/nchairun/data/mariadb

---

device: tells Docker where on my VM/host the MariaDB data should physically be stored.

---

container 💥
database files → still in /home/nchairun/data/mariadb ✅
new container → reconnects to them ✅

